#+TITLE: EQUAL ignores numeric fuzz inside lists and point coordinates
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: compatibility
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-29

* Reproduction and observation

Run the adjacent =equal-list-fuzz-repro.lsp= with:

#+begin_src sh
clautolisp --no-init --host cador --dialect bricscad-v26 \
  -l issues/open/equal-list-fuzz-repro.lsp
#+end_src

Observed using the locally available SBCL executable 2.3.73: scalar T,
list nil, outside-tolerance nil. The scalar and list use the exact same
numeric values and tolerance; only the container differs.

Source inspection of master b0942a5a (release source 2.3.78, not a newly
built binary): =builtin-equal= in
=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp= applies tolerance only
when both top-level operands are numbers; lists fall back to
=autolisp-equal-p= without passing tolerance.

* Actual caller and impact

SCHMS =src-vlx/geometry.lsp=, =schms_rotation_point=, returns a point.
The new pure test =src-vlx/test-unitaire-geometry.lsp= compares rotation
around (1 2 0) through a complete turn: error about 9e-16 is below 1e-9,
yet list EQUAL rejects the point. SCHMS keeps the product computation and
checks coordinates separately with the same 1e-9 bound. A negative control
rejects a point displaced by 0.01.

Autodesk explicitly documents fuzz for lists of real numbers, including
points: [[https://help.autodesk.com/cloudhelp/2024/DEU/AutoCAD-AutoLISP-Reference/files/GUID-7E85CB8F-B4DA-42F3-ABD3-89342A11EF9B.htm][equal (AutoLISP)]]. Point comparison with fuzz should accept coordinates
within the bound. Native qualification of this exact reproducer is still
pending; do not claim this local observation is a native probe receipt.

* Acceptance criteria

Apply fuzz recursively to numeric leaves of proper lists, nested lists,
points and dotted pairs, while retaining strict structure, length and
non-numeric comparisons. Preserve the scalar behavior and add tests
inside/outside tolerance, zero tolerance, negative fuzz and invalid fuzz
according to the native contract. Qualify the minimal reproducer on both
AutoCAD and BricsCAD.

The minimal reproducer uses decimal literals to avoid conflating EQUAL
with source-reader differences in scientific-notation token handling.

* Fixed 2026-10-29 -- clautolisp 2.3.81, branch fix-equal-list-fuzz

builtin-equal applied the fuzz only when both TOP-LEVEL operands were numbers;
lists fell back to autolisp-equal-p with no tolerance. New helper
autolisp-equal-fuzz-p (api.lisp) mirrors autolisp-equal-p but compares every
NUMERIC LEAF within +-tolerance, recursing through proper lists, nested lists,
points and both halves of a dotted pair; structure, length, strings and
non-numeric atoms stay strict (a number is never fuzz-equal to a non-number;
(5.0) is never equal to (5.0 5.0)). builtin-equal now routes BOTH the scalar and
the list case through it when fuzz is non-zero; nil / 0 fuzz still reduce to
strict autolisp-equal-p, and a non-number fuzz still signals. The scalar case is
just two numeric leaves, so old scalar behaviour is preserved.

Tests (builtin-apply-eval-eq-equal, fiveam 19/19): list inside/outside
tolerance, a 3-coordinate point, a nested list, a dotted pair, length kept
strict, non-numeric leaf strict with a fuzzed numeric sibling, and zero-fuzz =
exact (equal and differing). The minimal reproducer's three lines now all hold.

Remaining (native qualification, kept open): qualify the reproducer on AutoCAD
and BricsCAD, and pin NEGATIVE fuzz and invalid-fuzz against the vendor contract
-- clautolisp currently rejects everything under a negative tolerance (abs diff
>= 0 > negative) and signals on a non-number fuzz; neither is vendor-probed.
